{"lecture_id":"2026-07-21-M4-1","lecture_summary":"The lecture develops ideal mass–spring simple harmonic motion: sinusoidal position, velocity and acceleration, restoring force, amplitude, phase, period, frequency, angular frequency, and spring-system energy conservation. Its problem-solving examples include reading oscillation parameters from a graph, measuring displacement from a shifted equilibrium, evaluating position and velocity at a specified time, interpreting the motion from signs, and finding maximum speed using energy.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Simple Harmonic Motion, Mass Spring System - Amplitude, Frequency, Velocity - Physics Problems","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iubb3eFBQ9U","reason":"The strongest single-video match and therefore the smallest nonredundant set. It combines mass–spring problems with restoring force, sinusoidal motion, amplitude, period, frequency, velocity, acceleration, and energy, covering nearly all of the lecture’s core theory and calculation strategies."}],"coverage_gaps":["The one-dimensional projection of uniform circular motion as a geometric model of SHM has no strong explicit match.","The distinction between a localized oscillation and a propagating wave—including coupled oscillators and equality of source and wave frequency—has no focused match; the available wave videos primarily cover later wave mechanics.","The playlist descriptions do not explicitly indicate detailed treatment of the phase constant, sine–cosine phase shifts, or slope and phase relationships among position, velocity, and acceleration graphs.","No match explicitly reproduces the lecture’s shifted-equilibrium modeling workflow or its graph-reading example.","The derivation of spring potential energy from F = -dU/dx is not explicitly represented."]}
